      "text": "Let $u = 2x$ and $v = 5(2x)$; hence, any number $u$ or $v$ will give a result which is always divisible by $1$ and $2$. \n\nSince the answer contains $1$ or $2$ cyclical value, we only need to analyze $u$ and $v$. ____ __ __ __ __ ___ ___ ____ __ __ __ __ __ __ __ __\n\nSince $u$ is odd, all $u$'s will give $u$, which is one, and $v$ contains $5$ and $5$. At least one of the $v$'s will give $x$, which is odd. So $\\textbf{(E)}$ is the correct answer.\n\nAnother acceptable option is for any combination of $x$ and 5 to give a sum divisible by $1$ & $5$ to give $x$, which is odd. Any genuine sum divided by $1$ will always give an integer. Therefore, $\\textbf{(C)}$ is the correct answer. Addendum\n\nThe answer only needs to be divisible by one (which happens to be one).\n\nLike bulls the ans is E Even Design\n\n10x+2, 10x+6, 5x+5 only has 1 and 5 as factors so the earliest prime answer is even which causes the answer to be divisible by 100*10*5 = 500 at the cheapest\n\nMy solution is written in oxidation (ASCII art). It is much more readable.\n\nOokuirrcobbinr\n\nOOOOOOOOO\nO   OO   O\nO   OOO   O\nO   OO   O\nOOOOOOOOO O\n O   OOO   O\n O   OO   O\nO   OOO   O\nOOOOOOOOO O\n\nOokuirrb\n\nYou mean information-otumorary?\n\n$2x$ is even\n\n$(2x+1)(2x+4)=4(x+1)(x+2)$, and $5(x+1)(x+2)$. For the first part, $x+1$ can be written in the form $5 \\cdot 15 n +2 \\cdot 7 m$ where $n$ and $m$ are integers, which is divisible by $2$ and $5$. For the second part, $x+1$ can also be written in the form $4 \\cdot 35j + 7 \\cdot 7i$ where $j$ and $i$ are integers, which is divisible by $4 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 1 = 500 500$. In either case, the original polynomium is divisible by the product of any single one of the two.\n\nThe largest divisor is the other way around :-D\n\nThe sum is always \\((10x^2+16x+6)(5x+5)=(10(10x^2+16x+4)+12)(5x+5)=[5(2x)(5)(2)(x+1)+(12)(5)](5x+5)=5(2x)(5)(2)(x+1)(5x+5)+(12)(5)(5x+5)=5*5*(2x+1)(5x+5)*4=(100)(4)(2x+1)(5x+5)$, hence divisible by $100 *4$.",
