      "text": "To determine the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) when \\(x\\) is an odd number, we will first analyze the expression in simpler forms using algebraic identities and properties of odd numbers.\n\nFirst, note that since \\(x\\) is odd, we can write \\(10x = 5(2x)\\). Thus, \\(10x\\) can be expressed as an odd multiple of some integer (\\(5(2x)\\)).\n\nNext, let's consider the factors \\(10x + 2\\) and \\(10x + 6\\):\n1. \\(10x + 2 = 5(2x + 1)\\), which is clearly an odd multiple of 5.\n2. \\(10x + 6 = 2(5x + 3)\\), which is also an odd multiple of 2.\n\nNow, we multiply these by \\(5x + 5\\):\n\\[\n(10x + 2)(10x + 6)(5x + 5)\n\\]\nWe can rewrite this expression in terms of odd factors:\n\\[\n(10x + 2)(10x + 6)(5x + 5) = (5(2x + 1))(2(5x + 3))(5(x + 1))\n\\]\n\nNow, notice that the product includes:\n1. \\(5: 5(2x + 1)\\), \\(2(5x + 3)\\), and \\(5(x + 1)\\)\n2. A factor of 2 from each of the two terms involving 2.\n\nThus, the overall expression ensures that the product will always be divisible by:\n\\[\n5 \\times 2^2 = 20\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the largest integer that always divides \\( (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) \\) when \\( x \\) is an odd number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{20}\n\\]",
